WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.002 --> 00:00:03.962
In this low-lying car I drive in

00:00:03.962 --> 00:00:05.672
I've been noticing something lately.

00:00:05.839 --> 00:00:07.257
When I'm sitting in traffic

00:00:07.257 --> 00:00:09.884
I either can't see past 
the cars in front of me...

00:00:10.176 --> 00:00:12.971
or the cars around me 
are towering over me.

00:00:13.346 --> 00:00:15.724
And it turns out there's 
a big reason for that.

00:00:16.057 --> 00:00:18.685
The production of passenger cars 
like sedans and wagons

00:00:18.685 --> 00:00:22.272
for sale in the US, has been in freefall 
since 1975...

00:00:22.480 --> 00:00:24.607
and in their place has been 
the steady growth

00:00:24.607 --> 00:00:26.401
in the production of SUVs.

00:00:26.776 --> 00:00:30.905
Last year, SUVs and trucks made up 80% 
of all new car sales.

00:00:31.239 --> 00:00:33.950
Compare that to 52% in 2011.

00:00:34.159 --> 00:00:36.077
The fact that Americans like big cars 

00:00:36.077 --> 00:00:37.662
probably won't shock anyone.

00:00:37.787 --> 00:00:38.830
They are everywhere.

00:00:38.830 --> 00:00:41.041
Even in my parking scarce neighborhood 
in Brooklyn.

00:00:41.916 --> 00:00:43.710
But the reasons behind this transformation

00:00:43.710 --> 00:00:45.253
are more than just cultural.

00:00:45.545 --> 00:00:47.922
It all goes back to a 50-year-old policy

00:00:47.922 --> 00:00:51.217
that would kick off a huge shift 
in the way US cars are designed.

00:00:55.805 --> 00:00:57.640
So I'm driving from my home 
in Brooklyn, New York

00:00:57.640 --> 00:01:00.018
to a beach in Rhode Island for 
a summer vacation.

00:01:00.268 --> 00:01:02.353
And along the way, I'm going to 
piece together

00:01:02.353 --> 00:01:04.814
why big cars took over America.

00:01:06.733 --> 00:01:08.401
New York was the 45th state

00:01:08.401 --> 00:01:12.113
to officially succumb to the takeover 
of SUVs and trucks in 2014

00:01:12.113 --> 00:01:14.365
according to this Washington Post analysis.

00:01:14.532 --> 00:01:18.036
Alaska was the first state to go 
big-car dominant in 1988.

00:01:18.495 --> 00:01:20.455
So I'm wondering if you have any advice

00:01:20.455 --> 00:01:23.833
for things they should look out for 
on the road.

00:01:23.917 --> 00:01:25.585
Anywhere that you stop

00:01:25.585 --> 00:01:29.589
count the number of SUVs versus 
the number of passenger cars.

00:01:30.090 --> 00:01:31.049
Okay.

00:01:31.049 --> 00:01:34.260
I’m in the Costco parking lot... 
filled with SUVs.

00:01:34.385 --> 00:01:36.596
One, two, three...

00:01:37.472 --> 00:01:39.349
...four, five...

00:01:39.766 --> 00:01:42.310
...six, seven...

00:01:43.061 --> 00:01:47.941
...eight, nine, ten, 
11 SUVs in a row.

00:01:47.941 --> 00:01:49.901
I think that is my new record.

00:01:50.985 --> 00:01:53.113
Once you learn about how much

00:01:53.113 --> 00:01:56.741
big cars dominate the road, 
it's like you can't unsee it.

00:01:57.158 --> 00:01:59.744
People have lots of reasons 
for choosing a big car.

00:01:59.744 --> 00:02:02.622
And certainly the infrastructure 
in the US supports that choice.

00:02:02.747 --> 00:02:04.165
Unlike a lot of other countries

00:02:04.165 --> 00:02:06.459
our built environment revolves 
around cars.

00:02:06.918 --> 00:02:09.879
We have wide roads and wide, 
plentiful parking spaces

00:02:09.879 --> 00:02:11.673
and homes with plenty of parking.

00:02:12.090 --> 00:02:13.758
I arrived in Connecticut

00:02:13.758 --> 00:02:17.095
a state that succumbed to 
light truck dominance in 2016...

00:02:17.470 --> 00:02:18.638
and pulled into a rest stop

00:02:18.638 --> 00:02:20.265
where I interviewed Thomas Bochenek

00:02:20.515 --> 00:02:22.517
who was on a cross-country road trip
with his wife.

00:02:23.073 --> 00:02:25.186
It's very comfortable to drive.

00:02:25.186 --> 00:02:27.063
Well, I think the bigger the car, 
the more you...

00:02:27.188 --> 00:02:29.566
you have a feeling of security.

00:02:29.983 --> 00:02:32.152
You know, the smaller the car, 
you know...

00:02:33.027 --> 00:02:35.780
the more likely I think about 
getting into an accident

00:02:35.780 --> 00:02:36.447
and being hurt.

00:02:36.698 --> 00:02:41.202
It has no trouble pulling this 
almost 7,000 pound (3,175 kg) trailer.

00:02:43.663 --> 00:02:44.581
In other countries

00:02:44.581 --> 00:02:46.708
one deterrent for big cars 
is that they cost

00:02:46.708 --> 00:02:48.251
so much to fill up 
with gas.

00:02:48.376 --> 00:02:50.920
But due to low taxes 
on gas in the US...

00:02:51.254 --> 00:02:54.132
fuel is extremely cheap compared 
to other countries.

00:02:54.757 --> 00:02:56.593
But there's another overlooked bias towards

00:02:56.593 --> 00:02:59.179
big cars related to why SUVs exist

00:02:59.179 --> 00:03:00.388
in the first place.

00:03:02.974 --> 00:03:03.975
In the 1970s

00:03:03.975 --> 00:03:06.436
there was a shortage of 
foreign oil in the US.

00:03:06.728 --> 00:03:10.940
“1973 dramatized US dependence 
on foreign oil.”

00:03:11.274 --> 00:03:13.484
So the US government set rules
for automakers

00:03:13.484 --> 00:03:15.320
to start making cars 
more fuel efficient

00:03:15.320 --> 00:03:18.489
to lessen our dependance on the whims 
of the global oil market.

00:03:18.907 --> 00:03:23.369
By 1985, new cars had to get 
27.5 miles per gallon

00:03:23.369 --> 00:03:25.496
or roughly double their fuel efficiency.

00:03:25.788 --> 00:03:29.250
These new rules applied to cars 
like sedans and station wagons

00:03:29.250 --> 00:03:31.836
under a category known as passenger cars.

00:03:32.086 --> 00:03:34.422
But there were types of vehicles 
the US government exempted

00:03:34.422 --> 00:03:35.506
from these rules.

00:03:35.506 --> 00:03:36.883
Things like pickup trucks

00:03:36.883 --> 00:03:39.469
which everyday drivers weren't really 
driving at the time.

00:03:39.761 --> 00:03:42.222
And the idea was if these 
are working vehicles

00:03:42.222 --> 00:03:45.600
used by farmers, construction crews

00:03:45.600 --> 00:03:47.602
companies that are hauling freight...

00:03:48.061 --> 00:03:51.606
those vehicles need to be able 
to do their job

00:03:51.606 --> 00:03:54.692
without being choked by 
emissions controls

00:03:54.692 --> 00:03:58.029
and without being held to unrealistic 
fuel economy standards.

00:03:58.363 --> 00:04:01.032
So passenger cars got one set 
of strict rules...

00:04:01.157 --> 00:04:03.409
and this other category 
called light trucks...

00:04:03.576 --> 00:04:05.787
got another set of more relaxed standards.

00:04:05.995 --> 00:04:08.665
This regulation created an incentive for carmakers

00:04:08.665 --> 00:04:11.876
to transform light trucks into 
a vehicle for everyday use.

00:04:12.043 --> 00:04:14.504
Put in a radio, lots of 
cargo space

00:04:14.504 --> 00:04:16.756
and comfy seats and voila.

00:04:16.923 --> 00:04:18.758
That's how we got the SUV.

00:04:19.175 --> 00:04:22.095
“Okay, girls, we’re off in our 
new Jeep Cherokee.”

00:04:22.178 --> 00:04:24.973
“Behind that classic appearance, 
it's rugged and powerful.”

00:04:25.181 --> 00:04:28.268
An automaker needs to spend less money

00:04:28.268 --> 00:04:32.897
and less time on fuel economy 
and emissions improvement

00:04:32.897 --> 00:04:35.024
if they're making and selling an SUV

00:04:35.024 --> 00:04:36.818
because it falls under a category

00:04:36.818 --> 00:04:38.528
that is less stringently regulated.

00:04:38.778 --> 00:04:40.905
We had the Wagoneer, but 
it was very low volume

00:04:40.905 --> 00:04:42.782
very bespoke, very expensive.

00:04:42.782 --> 00:04:44.450
So only very few people bought it.

00:04:44.492 --> 00:04:46.327
People who lived in Manhattan 
would go to the Hamptons

00:04:46.327 --> 00:04:49.038
or go to northern New York State 
to go skiing.

00:04:49.122 --> 00:04:51.249
And that's how the SUV 
started off life.

00:04:51.582 --> 00:04:54.252
This is Ralph Gilles, head of design
at Stellantis

00:04:54.252 --> 00:04:57.046
a conglomerate of car companies 
that includes Jeep.

00:04:57.338 --> 00:05:00.049
One of the early SUVs was
the Chevy Blazer...

00:05:00.174 --> 00:05:03.136
built on the frame of the 
Chevy S-10 truck.

00:05:03.303 --> 00:05:05.638
This is the traditional definition of an SUV.

00:05:05.763 --> 00:05:07.682
If it was built on the frame of a truck.

00:05:08.016 --> 00:05:10.184
And to meet the legal definition 
of a light truck

00:05:10.184 --> 00:05:13.563
it had to have some clearance angles 
and dimensions just right.

00:05:13.813 --> 00:05:17.025
Taking a utilitarian vehicle 
and turning it into a...

00:05:17.734 --> 00:05:20.653
almost a premium luxury device
that happened to be utilitarian.

00:05:20.695 --> 00:05:21.988
Then comes the Grand Cherokee.

00:05:22.238 --> 00:05:23.531
The Jeep Grand Cherokee was built

00:05:23.531 --> 00:05:26.909
the way passenger cars are: 
in one piece, or unibody.

00:05:27.285 --> 00:05:30.913
These two types of car construction,
frame based and unibody...

00:05:31.289 --> 00:05:33.958
today form the distinction between 
traditional SUVs

00:05:33.958 --> 00:05:37.295
and the new, increasingly popular 
category of crossover.

00:05:37.587 --> 00:05:38.796
They're more efficiently packaged.

00:05:38.796 --> 00:05:39.756
You don't have the frame.

00:05:39.756 --> 00:05:41.966
The Grand Cherokee was part of 
the early success story

00:05:41.966 --> 00:05:44.635
story of SUVs in the 90s 
and 2000s. 

00:05:44.844 --> 00:05:46.429
One that continues today.

00:05:47.055 --> 00:05:50.266
This bias towards light trucks 
is why a company like Volkswagen

00:05:50.266 --> 00:05:52.643
which once sold primarily passenger cars

00:05:52.643 --> 00:05:54.854
has discontinued a lot of them today...

00:05:54.937 --> 00:05:57.982
like the Passat in favor of SUVs and crossovers.

00:05:58.107 --> 00:06:00.526
And they've stopped producing 
all of their wagons...

00:06:00.735 --> 00:06:02.695
including the Jetta Wagon I’m in.

00:06:03.029 --> 00:06:05.114
And a company like Ford has stopped

00:06:05.114 --> 00:06:07.492
producing all of their passenger cars except

00:06:07.492 --> 00:06:09.285
for the iconic Mustang.

00:06:12.538 --> 00:06:13.247
Okay.

00:06:13.247 --> 00:06:18.169
I'm going to see if anyone at this 
car dealership will talk to me.

00:06:18.795 --> 00:06:20.505
Buick was always car, really.

00:06:20.505 --> 00:06:21.798
That was mostly sedans.

00:06:22.090 --> 00:06:23.633
And then just with the market shift

00:06:23.633 --> 00:06:25.760
we actually don't even sell 
a Buick sedan anymore.

00:06:25.760 --> 00:06:27.053
They don't even make a Buick sedan.

00:06:27.345 --> 00:06:29.097
It's all crossovers.

00:06:29.305 --> 00:06:30.932
When was the last time you guys carried, like,

00:06:30.973 --> 00:06:32.183
a Buick passenger car?

00:06:32.266 --> 00:06:34.394
Oh, it was like three or four 
years ago.

00:06:34.560 --> 00:06:36.396
And how many did you have 
on the lot?

00:06:36.646 --> 00:06:39.357
Like 1 to 100 of, you know, SUVs.

00:06:39.357 --> 00:06:41.442
We're here to sell cars. We want 
to sell what people want.

00:06:41.484 --> 00:06:44.737
If they were here, if people didn't want them, 
I'm glad they're not here anymore, you know?

00:06:44.946 --> 00:06:47.031
So this chart starts to make a lot more sense.

00:06:47.532 --> 00:06:50.284
1975 was the year these 
fuel economy standards

00:06:50.284 --> 00:06:52.245
for sedans and wagons were passed...

00:06:52.537 --> 00:06:56.040
the same ones that didn't apply as strictly 
to SUVs and trucks.

00:06:56.541 --> 00:06:59.710
It's a little bit of a chicken and egg thing

00:06:59.710 --> 00:07:03.172
because the automakers have been 
marketing SUVs

00:07:03.172 --> 00:07:05.800
more preferentially than passenger cars.

00:07:05.842 --> 00:07:08.136
They've been putting more research and development

00:07:08.136 --> 00:07:10.680
into creating more models

00:07:10.680 --> 00:07:12.765
that fit into the SUV category.

00:07:13.141 --> 00:07:17.603
And they've made it so that it's 
an easier decision

00:07:17.603 --> 00:07:21.357
to get into an SUV than it is to 
get into a passenger car.

00:07:21.649 --> 00:07:24.527
As of 2016, our fuel economy standards shifted.

00:07:24.777 --> 00:07:27.238
Vehicles are still separated by 
the stricter standard

00:07:27.238 --> 00:07:30.366
for passenger cars and looser standard 
for light trucks.

00:07:30.950 --> 00:07:33.578
But within those categories, 
there are more breakdowns.

00:07:33.870 --> 00:07:36.247
Regulators multiply a car's wheelbase length

00:07:36.247 --> 00:07:38.040
by its width to get a footprint.

00:07:38.583 --> 00:07:41.586
And larger cars within each category 
get weaker rules.

00:07:42.128 --> 00:07:44.547
That means that carmakers are 
not only incentivized

00:07:44.547 --> 00:07:46.466
to phase out passenger cars 
in their fleet...

00:07:46.757 --> 00:07:49.010
but they are pushed to make 
each category bigger.

00:07:49.343 --> 00:07:52.221
The Toyota Camry got both 
longer and wider...

00:07:52.513 --> 00:07:54.474
and the Toyota RAV4 did, too.

00:07:55.224 --> 00:07:56.684
I've now reached Rhode Island...

00:07:57.143 --> 00:07:59.937
the very last state in the country
to go light truck dominant.

00:08:00.354 --> 00:08:04.734
We have started the inevitable transition
to electric vehicles but...

00:08:04.984 --> 00:08:07.612
You're still talking about a larger vehicle

00:08:07.612 --> 00:08:10.740
with higher ground clearance, 
that's heavier and taller.

00:08:11.073 --> 00:08:13.075
It's still going to be less...

00:08:13.576 --> 00:08:14.869
energy efficient.

00:08:14.869 --> 00:08:16.496
Whether it's an electric car

00:08:16.496 --> 00:08:18.372
or an internal combustion 
powered car.

00:08:18.414 --> 00:08:20.082
One sobering impact of big cars

00:08:20.082 --> 00:08:22.293
is the threat it poses to pedestrians.

00:08:22.835 --> 00:08:26.506
One study found that replacing 
the growth in SUVs with cars

00:08:26.506 --> 00:08:29.634
would have averted over 
a thousand pedestrian deaths.

00:08:29.926 --> 00:08:31.677
People on foot get hit higher

00:08:31.677 --> 00:08:35.056
and in more vulnerable regions 
when hit by an SUV...

00:08:35.264 --> 00:08:37.767
or a truck rather than a passenger car.

00:08:39.435 --> 00:08:42.855
Before hitting the beach, I stopped 
at my friend Kevin Wright's house.

00:08:43.105 --> 00:08:45.816
He’s bucked the SUV trend 
and drives a Honda Fit...

00:08:46.025 --> 00:08:48.361
a car that was discontinued in 2020.

00:08:48.569 --> 00:08:50.696
We had our daughter about 17 months ago...

00:08:50.821 --> 00:08:54.325
just kind of making sure that 
everybody has enough space

00:08:54.325 --> 00:08:57.161
to be comfortable as well as being safe.

00:08:57.161 --> 00:09:00.122
It gets good gas mileage and 
thinking about long term...

00:09:00.331 --> 00:09:02.375
you know, climate change is happening.

00:09:02.375 --> 00:09:04.335
What can we do to minimize 

00:09:04.335 --> 00:09:06.504
our carbon footprint 
as much as we can?

00:09:06.546 --> 00:09:09.382
So we were thinking about the second car...

00:09:09.423 --> 00:09:11.008
you know, maybe getting an SUV

00:09:11.008 --> 00:09:13.678
in that situation, but ultimately decided 
to kind of go

00:09:13.678 --> 00:09:16.556
the other direction and and got an e-bike.

00:09:18.975 --> 00:09:20.184
You have arrived.

00:09:23.396 --> 00:09:25.106
Even though we're in Rhode Island

00:09:25.106 --> 00:09:28.734
which was the last holdout 
for SUV dominance

00:09:28.734 --> 00:09:31.862
there are still a lot of SUVs here.

00:09:33.197 --> 00:09:35.283
I'm curious if you think there's 
any political will

00:09:35.283 --> 00:09:38.327
or urgency to change this

00:09:38.327 --> 00:09:40.496
trend towards bigger cars.

00:09:40.830 --> 00:09:42.665
That's you know, that's something that's—

00:09:42.665 --> 00:09:44.875
that has environmental concerns.

00:09:44.875 --> 00:09:46.669
That’s something that has economic concerns

00:09:46.669 --> 00:09:49.255
with how much we are reliant on oil.

00:09:49.338 --> 00:09:51.173
It also has to do with safety.

00:09:51.173 --> 00:09:54.010
You know, pedestrian safety, cyclists safety.

00:09:54.010 --> 00:09:57.680
So there is a desire among the younger

00:09:57.680 --> 00:10:00.933
people and more politically active people
to try to make it

00:10:00.933 --> 00:10:03.477
so that an SUV is not the default

00:10:03.477 --> 00:10:05.396
vehicle choice for American families.

00:10:06.022 --> 00:10:09.859
The US is unlikely to become a 
small car country any time soon.

00:10:09.900 --> 00:10:12.278
But this dramatic transition to big cars

00:10:12.278 --> 00:10:14.196
wasn't just about consumer choice.

00:10:14.572 --> 00:10:16.407
It was enabled by a policy choice.

00:10:16.907 --> 00:10:18.909
So if we want our roads to look different...

00:10:19.201 --> 00:10:20.911
we can try and start there.

